{"name":"Crispy Gold","hex":"#c49832","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crispy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a crisp texture; brittle yet tender.","Denoting something is impressive or desirable; cool, awesome; fresh."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The well-baked fat on the surface of a piece of roasted meat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crispy"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crispiness","blocks":[{"text":"Crispiness or crispness is one of the most common food texture attributes. Crispiness refers to a hard food that emits a sound upon fracturing. Foods described as crisp tend not to show signs of deformation prior to fracture. Crispiness and crunchiness are often used interchangeably, however crispiness tends to be associated with a higher pitched sound, while crunchiness is associated with lower pitched sounds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Pernil.1.jpg/330px-Pernil.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crispiness","wikidata":"Q5186058"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/3